  A task therefore notifies at most twice across its life, once per state, because [38;5;153mkey[39m includes [38;5;153mkind[39m. When it crosses
  from [38;5;153mdue[39m into [38;5;153moverdue[39m, the [38;5;153mdue[39m entry simply stops being computed and the ledger prunes its stale key — no transition
  code, it falls out of the derivation.

  [1mExclusions.[0m [38;5;153mdueAt === null[39m never notifies. [38;5;153mdone === true[39m never notifies, and completing a task prunes its ledger
  entries, so re-opening a completed task can notify again. That last bit is a deliberate choice: re-opening means you
  care again.

  [1mMissed transitions.[0m Laptop sleep, closed tab, clock jumps — none need special handling. A tick computes the state from
  [38;5;153mnow[39m; if a task is overdue and [38;5;153moverdue:<id>[39m isn't in the ledger, it delivers. You get the notification late rather
  than never, which is right. The one hazard is the [1mburst[0m: first load with a backlog of stale tasks would fire a dozen
  toasts at once. I'd cap OS toasts at 3 per tick, with the remainder appearing in the in-page list only and still
  marked delivered.

  [1mPermission flow.[0m Never prompt on page load — that's the pattern browsers added prompt-blocking for. Instead:

  - [38;5;153mNotification[39m absent, or non-secure context → feature simply doesn't exist; in-page only, no UI for it
  - [38;5;153mpermission === 'default'[39m → an "Enable desktop notifications" row in the bell dropdown; prompt only on that click
  - [38;5;153mgranted[39m → toast, capped as above
  - [38;5;153mdenied[39m → affordance hidden permanently, in-page list unaffected, no nagging

  Every branch here degrades to the in-page list, which is why in-page is the source of truth rather than a fallback
  bolted on.

  [1mRead state.[0m The badge counts computed notifications whose ledger entry has [38;5;153mreadAt === null[39m. Opening the dropdown marks
  everything currently listed as read — standard bell behavior, and it means "unread" tracks [3mwhat you haven't looked [0m
  [3mat[0m, not [3mwhat you haven't dismissed[0m. No separate dismiss action; a notification leaves the list when the underlying
  task is done, deleted, or edited to a new due date.

  Two things I picked a default for rather than asking, flag them if you disagree: the 24h lead time, and
  open-marks-all-read.

  Does Section 2 look right?

